data.description:
Why: both client kinds (user ruling): harness code speaks JSONL over stdio; an agent in a shell needs a named, persistent session it can reach from separate commands.

What: `serve` in stdio mode and socket mode. One Go package of request and response structs is shared by server and client, per the house rule on serialization seams. Requirements:
- stdout carries protocol only, diagnostics go to stderr;
- every request has an id and a structured response;
- every run has a budget, with a default;
- inspection never advances emulation.

Sessions are named, listable and prunable, with an idle timeout. An append-only JSONL event log records each session's requests and results, and the gap ledger (counts of *unsupported* answers by capability) is part of it.

Why: the user wants the bespoke core kept a real option, knowing that a working wrapper builds momentum. This is the scheduled decision point (project/2026-11-06-backend-evaluation.md, The bespoke core question).

What: decide with the user, from evidence:
- the gap-ledger counts;
- eval stumbles caused by core limits;
- SameBoy's shootout failures (from the corpus leaf);
- any fidelity issues found.

Options:
(a) Spike now. Rust, implementing the same core C interface; DMG only, no APU. Checkpoint 1, about 2 agent sessions: blargg cpu_instrs and instr_timing, plus instruction-level lockstep with SameBoy. Checkpoint 2, about 3 more: mem_timing, mooneye timer, interrupt and OAM DMA tests, dmg-acid2 pixel-exact, frame-level lockstep across homebrew ROMs. Gate: at least SameBoy's pass list on that set, plus a cost projection for the APU and CGB tail. Stop at about 6 sessions either way.
(b) Park it in project/backlog.md with a written un-park condition.

Record the decision in a dated doc.

